    "abstract": [
     "Background: Synchrony is a core mechanism through which parents help regulate children’s arousal. By matching their emotional tone to the child’s, parents convey validation, mutuality, and support for managing distress. Although synchrony is widely recognized as an essential part of parent-child interaction, little is known about how it functions in contexts that are dysregulating for both partners, such as shared traumatic experiences. In the study, we examined whether parent and adolescent posttraumatic stress symptoms (PTSS) following the October 7th events were related to the development of acoustic synchrony during a dyadic conversation, and whether synchrony, in turn, contributed to a reduction in adolescents’ PTSS over time. Method: Fifty-six Jewish Israeli parent-adolescent dyads engaged in a recorded conversation on adolescents’ exposure to war-related content on digital media. We assessed parent and child PTSS before the conversation and three months after. The conversations were audio-recorded and encoded for mean pitch (f0) and speech rate. Synchrony was calculated as the change in standardized differences in speech rate and mean pitch from the beginning to the end of the conversation. Results and conclusions: The study results revealed that adolescents’, but not parents’, higher pre-conversation PTSS predicted reduced speech rate convergence. Contrary to expectations, greater speech rate convergence predicted increases in adolescents’ PTSS over time. These findings suggest that under conditions of shared traumatic stress, synchrony may be more challenging to achieve and may not uniformly support regulation; instead, when emotional dysregulation is high, synchrony may amplify distress for both partners rather than attenuate it."
    ]
