     "We developed the Puppet Theater Value Task, in which children make simple binary choices between contrasting values, with each possible pairing presented at least twice. Israeli Jewish and Arab children aged 1.5–6 years (N = 390) watched short scenarios in which two teddy bears expressed competing preferences—for example, “I want all the kids to get the same amount of candy” (self-transcendence) versus “I want to get a lot of candy, more than other kids get” (self-enhancement)—and selected the option they preferred. Coherence was assessed using the Condorcet method, whereby preferences are considered consistent when pairwise choices form a transitive ordering rather than cyclical patterns.",
     "Children younger than three frequently did not complete the task, and data quality improved with age. Among 321 children with valid responses, 68% showed choice patterns that were more coherent than chance, with coherence increasing from near-random levels at age three to substantially greater consistency by age six. Other-oriented values were generally prioritized, with significant differences between Arab and Jewish children. These findings provide the first evidence that meaningful, structured value systems can emerge before age five and suggest that socially oriented values are already highly important in early childhood."
