     "When children watch the same movie, their brains in part respond in shared ways. Measuring that shared response reveals which regions track the experience and how individual children differ — differences that may serve as developmental or clinical markers. The standard tool, intersubject correlation, tests thousands of brain locations separately, which makes false discoveries hard to control. It also reports only how strongly children’s responses agree at each location; the shape of the shared response over the course of the movie is not among its outputs and must be recovered in a second, separate analysis.",
