     "Background: Children with congenital heart disease (CHD) may experience differences in multiple dimensions of health-related quality of life (HRQoL). Although survival has improved substantially, evidence regarding HRQoL in children with CHD compared with their healthy siblings remains inconsistent.",
     "Objectives: To quantitatively synthesize differences in HRQoL between children with CHD and their healthy siblings, and to examine potential moderators.",
     "Methods: A systematic review and meta-analysis were conducted according to PRISMA guidelines. Six observational studies comprising 1,561 participants (799 children with CHD and 762 healthy siblings), aged 1-25 years, were included. All studies assessed HRQoL using the PedsQL. Random-effects meta-analysis was used to estimate pooled standardized mean differences (Hedges’ g) in overall HRQoL and specific PedsQL domains. Random-effects meta-regression analyses examined potential moderators.",
     "Results: Children with CHD had significantly lower overall HRQoL than their healthy siblings (Hedges’ g = 0.512, 95% CI [0.161, 0.862], p = .004). Significant deficits were consistently observed across physical, emotional, social, school, and psychosocial domains. Meta-regression indicated that the HRQoL disparity decreased with increasing mean age and a higher proportion of females, whereas the disparity increased with higher proportions of moderate and severe CHD.",
