     "Purpose: This study examined whether delays in specific gross motor milestones during infancy were differentially associated with later language outcome profiles among infants with motor developmental delay (MDD).",
     "Method: Electronic health records from a child development institute in Israel were used in this retrospective longitudinal study. The final sample included 285 children with MDD who had standardized gross motor assessment during infancy and later standardized language assessment between 2 and 8 years of age. Gross motor milestones included rolling, independent sitting, hands-and-knees crawling, pull-to-stand, cruising, and independent walking. Language outcomes were classified as language within normal limits, expressive-only language impairment (LI), or combined receptive-expressive LI. Univariable and multivariable logistic regression models examined associations between delayed motor milestones and later language outcomes.",
